1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:04,494
The S three bucket was public for eleven days.

2
00:00:04,494 --> 00:00:07,000
The pull request had two approvals.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:09,148
Nobody read line two fourteen.

4
00:00:12,148 --> 00:00:15,866
What if every configuration change got a reviewer that

5
00:00:15,866 --> 00:00:18,070
never gets tired, never guesses,

6
00:00:18,070 --> 00:00:20,136
and always shows its evidence?

7
00:00:20,136 --> 00:00:21,926
In two days you build one.

8
00:00:24,926 --> 00:00:28,626
Gennoor Tech presents the Agentic Configuration Reviewer.

9
00:00:31,626 --> 00:00:35,938
Deterministic rules decide. The model explains and drafts the

10
00:00:35,938 --> 00:00:39,896
fix. Native tooling validates. And the pull request gets

11
00:00:39,896 --> 00:00:41,663
a comment, never a merge.

12
00:00:41,663 --> 00:00:44,773
That division of labour is the whole design,

13
00:00:44,773 --> 00:00:47,671
and it is why the reviewer can be trusted

14
00:00:47,671 --> 00:00:48,166
in C I.

15
00:00:51,166 --> 00:00:55,030
Day one starts with parsers for Terraform plan output

16
00:00:55,030 --> 00:00:58,237
and Kubernetes YAML, using native libraries,

17
00:00:58,237 --> 00:01:00,570
so every finding keeps its file,

18
00:01:00,570 --> 00:01:03,705
line and resource provenance. Then a small,

19
00:01:03,705 --> 00:01:07,642
honest ruleset: public exposure, privileged workloads,

20
00:01:07,642 --> 00:01:11,287
overly broad permissions. Each rule has a stable I

21
00:01:11,287 --> 00:01:16,025
D, severity, rationale, references, exceptions and test fixtures,

22
00:01:16,025 --> 00:01:20,326
including secure configurations to measure false positives.

23
00:01:23,326 --> 00:01:26,424
Then the agent. It receives the parsed resource and

24
00:01:26,424 --> 00:01:29,583
the rule evidence and returns a concise explanation,

25
00:01:29,583 --> 00:01:32,256
its uncertainty and a remediation rationale,

26
00:01:32,256 --> 00:01:35,050
in a strict schema. You verify that nothing it

27
00:01:35,050 --> 00:01:37,054
says can change a policy outcome.

28
00:01:40,054 --> 00:01:43,777
Day two is remediation. Minimal candidate diffs,

29
00:01:43,777 --> 00:01:48,197
only for supported cases, never touching unrelated files.

30
00:01:48,197 --> 00:01:50,989
Every patch runs through formatting,

31
00:01:50,989 --> 00:01:56,029
syntax validation, native tooling, policy checks and tests before

32
00:01:56,029 --> 00:01:59,054
it is allowed to call itself validated.

33
00:01:59,054 --> 00:02:02,543
You will watch an invalid patch get rejected.

34
00:02:05,543 --> 00:02:08,860
Then integration. A local review report first,

35
00:02:08,860 --> 00:02:13,041
then pull request comments in a sandbox repository through

36
00:02:13,041 --> 00:02:16,430
a C I workflow. The integration is read-only by

37
00:02:16,430 --> 00:02:20,251
default, handles untrusted repository content safely,

38
00:02:20,251 --> 00:02:23,711
and never merges. You finish with an evaluation:

39
00:02:23,711 --> 00:02:26,595
precision and recall on the fixture set,

40
00:02:26,595 --> 00:02:30,632
unsupported statements, patch validity and review noise.

41
00:02:33,632 --> 00:02:37,802
Labs run on a sandbox repository with seeded misconfigurations,

42
00:02:37,802 --> 00:02:41,044
so you can see real pull request comments appear,

43
00:02:41,044 --> 00:02:44,022
with evidence and a validated patch attached.

44
00:02:47,022 --> 00:02:49,554
Here is the full table of contents,

45
00:02:49,554 --> 00:02:51,289
6 modules over two days.

46
00:02:51,289 --> 00:02:54,978
Day one: Supported formats, parsers and provenance;

47
00:02:54,978 --> 00:02:59,245
Deterministic policy rules; and AI explanations in a strict

48
00:02:59,245 --> 00:03:03,223
schema. Day two: Candidate diffs and native validation;

49
00:03:03,223 --> 00:03:06,622
Pull-request integration, read-only by default;

50
00:03:06,622 --> 00:03:10,238
and Evaluation, developer experience and capstone.

51
00:03:13,238 --> 00:03:18,294
Every build in this course follows six engineering principles.

52
00:03:18,294 --> 00:03:20,496
Model output is a proposal,

53
00:03:20,496 --> 00:03:24,736
never a fact. Authorization lives outside the model.

54
00:03:24,736 --> 00:03:28,324
A human approves every consequential action.

55
00:03:28,324 --> 00:03:31,097
Decisions and evidence are logged.

56
00:03:31,097 --> 00:03:35,908
The stack stays provider-agnostic. And quality is measured,

57
00:03:35,908 --> 00:03:36,886
not assumed.

58
00:03:39,886 --> 00:03:42,397
Two days, hands-on. Two formats,

59
00:03:42,397 --> 00:03:45,772
Terraform and Kubernetes. Zero auto-merges,

60
00:03:45,772 --> 00:03:49,381
ever. One C I integrated reviewer you can take

61
00:03:49,381 --> 00:03:49,773
home.

62
00:03:52,773 --> 00:03:55,179
This course is for DevSecOps,

63
00:03:55,179 --> 00:04:00,487
platform and cloud engineers, infrastructure as code authors and

64
00:04:00,487 --> 00:04:04,137
